# Healer deactivated-data follow-up

The earlier valid-index/bounded-selection workaround is already removed in landed PR111. This is a different remaining edge: source NPC data is nulled before the clip detaches.

A controlled current-World crossing probe (`probe.js`/`probe.json`) creates an outgoing patient, kills it with an actual existing shell during that field's bullet loop, then runs an incoming Healer holding that patient. The dead patient remains in the outgoing raw list and still has a parent. Current selection retains it and a later incoming sentinel advances to age1.

Recovered control flow predicts a null-data read in Healer PartCallRun, followed by a second escaping read from the catch-side raw acquisition. The later sentinel should stay at age0 for that Main.Run frame. This expectation is source/pcode-derived, not an original-runtime execution result. Exact source hashes/ranges are in `source-audit.json`.

A faithful fix must preserve raw order/held object/parent lifetime and the exact catch boundary, rather than skipping dead targets. Direct HealerBeam AddLife also needs its increment-before-null-read order audited. No production files or published PR head were changed for this investigation. Publish subsequent fidelity work only after the final backlog PR and saved drag dependency are handled.


## Existing beam after queued patient death

`beam-null-probe.js` now reproduces a second ordering contradiction using the real World bullet loop and shell damage. The still-parented patient is queued before an existing beam with contact counter10 reaches it. Current code increments life to1, resets that counter to0, then advances the later sentinel and player. On the following frame it removes the patient/beam. The hit-test adapter deliberately supplies contact; this is not native artwork/contact verification.

HealerBeam calls AddLife before resetting its counter. AddLife increments intTankLife before reading objData.numLife; NPC Deactivate has already nulled objData. Source-derived behavior therefore keeps life1 and counter10 while aborting later Main.Run work. Do not replace this with an early dead-patient skip. These observations remain current-code execution plus source inference, with no original-runtime differential. The source digest and next-frame output are retained in source-audit.json and beam-null-probe.json.


## Existing beam after owner death or jam

`beam-owner-probe.js` creates the beam through `tickHealer` and records a healthy control, an actual earlier-shell owner kill, and explicit jam status. The healthy case heals to51. In both changed-owner cases the patient stays50 and the current beam is removed, despite the owner retaining a parent. Category6 is registered in `_‑JG`, whose tank deactivation loop invokes only inherited `Deactivate`, not `FinalPartCall`; the pre-existing beam keeps its own home/patient references. The source beam tests parent presence, and JammedMe does not stop it. Source-derived behavior therefore keeps that frame’s beam contact; dead-home detachment on the following frame remains the removal boundary. Exact paths/hashes, scope and limits are retained in source-audit.json. This is not an original-runtime comparison.


## Cached parent boundary: preserve the existing contract

A follow-up read of BTAGameWorld._-4j, _-0D.Deactivate/_-Zu/_-Yz and _-G0 rules out a proposed blanket on-stage target filter. Removing an old field wrapper leaves the cached clip’s enemy-layer parent and that layer’s wrapper parent intact. The Aggressor parent.parent check therefore does not equate to stage attachment; cached direct references and their unrooted coordinate conversions must remain supported. Reattachment reparents the same clip. New shots are also blocked during transition in both source and current code. This audit narrows the future Healer fix to the known data/death/jam call boundaries rather than changing retained-target ownership.
